<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cloaca Maxima</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cloaca Maxima ('Greatest Sewer') is one of the oldest sewer systems in the world, built in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to drain the marshy valley of the Forum into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>. Tradition attributes it to the Etruscan kings of the 6th century BCE; it began as an open channel and was later vaulted over, and parts of it are still in use.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cloaca-maxima.jpg" alt="Cloaca Maxima" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gradvmedusa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_downtown_Rome_during_the_Roman_Empire_large-annotated.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
